 "equations": [
  {
   "name": "Buffered output (Equation 1)",
   "expr": "VOUT+ = VIN",
   "where": {
    "VOUT+": "output of amplifier 1",
    "VIN": "single-ended input"
   },
   "note": "",
   "page": 10
  },
  {
   "name": "Inverting output (Equation 2)",
   "expr": "VOUT– = VREF × (R4 / (R3 + R4)) × (1 + R2 / R1) – VIN × R2 / R1",
   "where": {
    "VOUT–": "output of amplifier 2",
    "VREF": "reference voltage, 12 V",
    "R1": "input resistor of the inverting stage",
    "R2": "feedback resistor of the inverting stage",
    "R3": "upper resistor of the reference divider",
    "R4": "lower resistor of the reference divider"
   },
   "note": "",
   "page": 10
  },
  {
   "name": "Differential output (Equation 3)",
   "expr": "VDIFF = VOUT+ – VOUT– = VIN × (1 + R2 / R1) – VREF × (R4 / (R3 + R4)) × (1 + R2 / R1)",
   "where": {
    "VDIFF": "voltage between the two outputs"
   },
   "note": "",
   "page": 10
  },
  {
   "name": "Outputs with R1 = R2 and R3 = R4 (Equations 4 and 5)",
   "expr": "VOUT+ = VIN; VOUT– = VREF – VIN",
   "where": {},
   "note": "",
   "page": 10
  },
  {
   "name": "Differential output with R1 = R2 and R3 = R4 (Equation 6)",
   "expr": "VDIFF = 2 × VIN – VREF",
   "where": {},
   "note": "The largest input equals VREF and so does the largest output of each amplifier, giving a differential span of 2 × VREF.",
   "page": 10
  },
  {
   "name": "Common-mode output (Equation 7)",
   "expr": "Vcm = (VOUT+ + VOUT–) / 2 = VREF / 2",
   "where": {
    "Vcm": "average of the two outputs"
   },
   "note": "",
   "page": 10
  }
 ],
 "operation": [
  {
   "topic": "Family",
   "page": 1,
   "text": "The RC4580 is a dual op amp built for audio: low noise, wide gain-bandwidth, low distortion and enough output current to drive headphones, with a supply range wide enough for low-voltage use too. It is a drop-in for the NJM4580 and matches the pins and function of the LM833, NE5532, NJM4558/9 and NJM4560/2/5. It comes in SOIC (D), PDIP (P), TSSOP (PW), VSSOP (DGK) and SOT-23 (DDF), all 8 pins. An automotive grade, RC4580-Q1, also exists."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Inside each amplifier",
   "page": 8,
   "text": "Each amplifier is all bipolar: a differential input pair, a gain stage and an output stage, all between VCC+ and VCC–."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Bandwidth, CMRR and slew rate",
   "page": 8,
   "text": "Unity-gain bandwidth, the highest frequency a gain-of-one stage passes without heavy distortion, is 12 MHz. CMRR, the change in input offset voltage over the change in common-mode input expressed in decibels, is 110 dB. Slew rate, the fastest the output moves in answer to an input change, is 5 V/µs."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Power-up and supply",
   "page": 8,
   "text": "There is no enable: the amplifiers run as soon as supply is applied, from a single supply or from split supplies. Operation is specified from ±2 V to ±16 V, with many limits holding from –40°C to 125°C; beyond ±18 V the part can be permanently damaged. Several parameters shift noticeably with supply voltage and temperature, as the typical curves show."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Supply bypass",
   "page": 11,
   "text": "A 0.1 µF bypass capacitor at the supply pins keeps noise from a noisy or high-impedance supply out. With split supplies each supply pin gets its own low-ESR 0.1 µF ceramic to ground, as near the part as possible; with a single supply one capacitor from V+ to ground is enough."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Layout",
   "page": 12,
   "text": "Keep analog and digital grounds apart; ground planes on one or more layers of a multilayer board spread heat and cut EMI pickup. Route input traces short and away from supply and output traces, and where one must cross a noisy trace, cross at a right angle. Place the external parts close to the amplifier, RF and RG right at the inverting input to keep its stray capacitance small. A low-impedance driven guard ring around critical traces cuts leakage from nearby traces at other voltages."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Typical application: parts choice",
   "page": 10,
   "text": "In the single-ended to differential converter, DC linearity is set by the input common-mode range and the output swing, so an amplifier with inputs and output that reach the rails is generally wanted. VOUT– depends directly on R1 to R4, so tight-tolerance resistors keep its error small; the design used 36 kΩ parts measured within 2 %."
  }
 ],
